From 13fd8b4e3dd1e162254bc2ae68ef8505694f64d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:57:25 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui: show the agent's tasks in a floating card over the
 agent panel

0034 gave AcpThread the adapter's task feed -- every live task of a session,
how each one ended, and _claude/tasks/stop and /background over the
connection -- and rendered none of it. A task sent to the background outlives
the turn that launched it, so the transcript scrolls it away, and the only
trace left is 0029's sidebar spinner: it says that something runs, not what,
for how long, or how to stop it. This patch is the card that shows the tasks
and acts on them (story 013). The tray notification is 0038.

What the card is:

  * One TasksCard per AgentPanel, created with the panel before any thread,
    floating over the conversation in a corner of the panel. It lists the
    live tasks of every conversation the panel holds -- the active one and
    the retained ones -- grouped by thread: title and project once per group,
    the tasks beneath, each with its status, elapsed time (repainted every
    second while something runs), type, and a foreground/background chip. An
    ended task keeps its final status on the card for 10 seconds, then drops
    off. With nothing to show, the card is not drawn at all.

  * The card owns only what belongs to no thread: minimized, watched, ended
    rows still on show, per-row errors. The tasks are read live from each
    root AcpThread, so there is no copy to drift. Minimized, it becomes a
    pill carrying the running count. Disabled, hidden or minimized only stops
    painting: it keeps following every thread in every state.

  * Two settings, in default.json and settings_content:
    agent.tasks_card.enabled (default true) and agent.tasks_card.position
    (top_right, top_left, bottom_right or bottom_left; default top_right).
    It defaults to ON because it costs nothing until there is a task: with
    none, nothing is drawn.

  * Placement is measured at prepaint, in TasksCardOverlay, painted deferred
    so it sits over the panel's own floating controls. The card stays inside
    the conversation's viewport -- clear of the toolbar above and of the
    message editor's send, stop and model controls below -- or, for an empty
    thread, inside the editor's text area. A plain absolute div spilled past
    a small panel and covered exactly those controls.

The actions, each a Tab stop with a tooltip and an accessible label:

  * Stop asks first ("Stop this task?", naming it), then sends
    _claude/tasks/stop. A prompt dropped unanswered is a Cancel. A task that
    ended while the prompt was open is not the user's error, so its "is not
    running" answer shows nothing; any other failure is shown on the row with
    the whole error chain ({:#}), because the connection wraps the agent's
    own text under its context and the outer line alone says nothing.

  * Send to Background, offered only for a running foreground task that has
    a tool call id, sends _claude/tasks/background. It does not ask: the task
    keeps running, so nothing is lost. An answer that nothing moved is a row
    error, not a silent no-op.

  * Open brings the task's thread forward and scrolls to the tool call that
    started it; the group header opens at its oldest running task. The card
    holds no handle to the panel -- the panel owns it -- so it emits
    TasksCardEvent::OpenTask and the owning panel acts. A thread the panel
    does not hold is refused with a row error rather than loaded, which would
    start its agent as the side effect of a click. A nested subagent's tool
    call is not an entry of the root thread, so that task opens the thread
    without scrolling.

  * Watch shows a row's progress (tokens, tool uses, last tool, summary) and
    marks its ending as one the tray should hear about (0038). In memory
    only: a restart starts with nothing watched.

Three changes reach into upstream code, and each is needed by the above:

  * ConversationView's NewEntry handler syncs every entry not yet synced --
    EntryViewState::synced_entry_count()..len, the count being new -- instead
    of only len - 1. Entries pushed within one update reach the view as one
    event each, all flushed after the last push, so the old handler synced
    the last entry against a state missing the ones before it and indexed
    past its end: a panic, reached by opening a task's thread whose entries
    arrived in one batch. For one entry per flush it is identical; when
    nothing is pending it is a no-op instead of a duplicate splice.

  * AgentPanel::open_thread_by_session first reactivates a conversation the
    panel already holds, by its thread id. A held thread with no metadata row
    yet was otherwise reopened through the native agent as a second, empty
    conversation. It also changes 0024's deep link in that one case: a link
    to a thread that is already open now brings that thread forward.

  * remove_retained_thread takes cx and tells the card; the raw removal is
    split out as take_retained_thread, which test_unload_retained_thread now
    uses, and one upstream test call site gains its cx. The card is synced
    deferred and coalesced to the end of the effect cycle, because activating
    a thread removes its view and re-adds it, and an immediate sync read that
    gap as a closed thread and dropped its ended rows and watches.

The dock caches the panel's view, so a notify from the card alone never
repaints it: the panel observes the card and re-renders when the card shows
or hides; once shown, the card repaints itself. The two AgentSettings struct
literals in test modules (agent_ui, agent::tool_permissions) gain the new
field, as they did for 0027: they construct the struct exhaustively.

Rides the claude-agent-acp-plus flag BY DEPENDENCY, like 0011, 0013, 0029 and
0034: only this adapter sends _meta["_claude/tasks"], and without it the card
has nothing to list and draws nothing. It needs claude-agent-acp-plus 0.24.0
or later. The released 0.23.6 sends only 0029's _claude/backgroundTasks count,
never the snapshot -- measured live: two background sleeps ran and no card
appeared -- so until 0.24.0 is published and the ebuild requires it, this card
and 0034's feed beneath it are inert. Nothing breaks; nothing shows.

Test added 2026-10-09 (0.2.2_p20261009) for this patch's fix inside upstream's NewEntry
handler: three entries pushed in one thread update are all synced into the view,
and the next push lands on a caught-up view. Fails when the handler syncs only the
last index again.

Tests added (0.2.3_p20261009): a held conversation with no metadata row comes forward
instead of a second, empty one; a session with no row falls back to open_thread
(0024's fallback, tested here because it needs this patch's held-view check).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
